US-ASCII
- encodes 128 specified characters into seven-bit integers
- 95 characters are printable
- 33 non-printing control codes, 0..31
- transmission least significant bit first
- patterned most control codes together and all graphic codes together
- ESC usually indicates the start of a command sequence
- In C library and Unix conventions, the null character is used to terminate text strings
- ISO/IEC 646, like ASCII, is a 7-bit character set. It does not make any additional codes available, so the same code
- points encoded different characters in different countries.
- The IBM PC defined code page 437, which replaced the control characters with graphic symbols such as smiley faces.
- The ISO/IEC 8859 standard (derived from the DEC-MCS) finally provided a standard
- that most systems copied (at least as
- Windows-1252 (often mislabeled as ISO-8859-1) with typographic punctuation marks needed for traditional text printing
- ASCII was incorporated into the Unicode (1991) character set as the first 128 symbols, so the 7-bit ASCII characters
  have the same numeric codes in both sets. This allows UTF-8 to be backward compatible with 7-bit ASCII
- ANSI escape sequences are a standard for in-band signaling

